<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arwa al-Sulayhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1048–1138</span> · Also known as Arwa bint Ahmad, Queen Arwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arwa al-Sulayhi was queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayhid-dynasty">Sulayhid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, who governed for some fifty years, first for her ailing husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mukarram">Ahmad al-Mukarram</a> and then in her own right, until her death in 1138. She moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibla">Dhu Jibla</a>, built its great mosque and was given authority over the Fatimid Isma'ili mission in Yemen. In the Fatimid split of 1130 she backed al-Tayyib, founding the Tayyibi branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a>.</p></div>
